/P 151 0 R
/E (Kosher is the set of Jewish dietary laws that govern what and how Jews eat. They come from the Torah and are observed first and foremost because they are a divine commandment.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 151 0 R
/E (Only certain animals are permitted. Mammals must both have split hooves and chew their cud, so cows, sheep, goats, and deer are kosher, while pigs, rabbits, and horses are not. Domestic birds like chicken, duck, and turkey are kosher. Fish must have fins and scales, so salmon and tuna are kosher, while shellfish, catfish, and swordfish are not. Reptiles and insects are generally forbidden. Eggs and milk are kosher only if they come from kosher animals, and honey is permitted.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [32]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 151 0 R
/E (Meat must come from an animal slaughtered by a trained person called a shochet, using a swift, painless cut with a perfectly smooth knife. All blood must then be removed, through soaking and salting. Meat and dairy are never cooked or eaten together, so separate utensils, dishes, and surfaces ````````)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [33]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 151 0 R
/E (are used, and a waiting period is observed between eating them. Foods that are neither meat nor dairy, such as eggs, fruits, vegetables, and grains, are called pareve and can be eaten with either. Fish is also pareve, though it is not eaten with meat. Fruits and vegetables must be checked for insects, and wine and grape juice must be certified kosher.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [34]
>>
endobj
