333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Living Moshiach)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (You have no idea how huge an impact your Shabbos dinners are having. You are making history and fixing the world. And feeding your family too. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Shabbos dinner is a cosmic event, a journey from Creation to the coming of Moshiach. Each element adds another dimension and conveys a deep message.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
336 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (We start with Kiddush , recalling how G-d created the world in six days and rested on the seventh. The universe has a Creator, and He made it for a purpose.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Then we drink the wine, correcting the very first human mistake. Adam and Eve ate the forbidden fruit, which according to one opinion in the Talmud was a grape. They used the fruit for something forbidden. We use it for a mitzvah. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Next we wash our hands , taking us to Noah's Flood. The world had become so corrupt that it needed to be washed clean. But instead of waiting for a flood to purify us, we wash our own hands. We can clean up our own mess. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
339 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Then comes the challah dipped in salt . This reminds us of Sodom, a society so selfish that people wouldn't even share salt with a neighbour. We do the opposite. We put salt on our bread and share it generously with family, friends and guests. Where Sodom shut people out, our Shabbos table welcomes them in.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Response:)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Question:)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
346 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Not bad. We fixed the sin of Adam and Eve, the generation of the Flood and Sodom, and we haven't even reached the first course.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
347 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (So then comes the meal itself . We enter the world of food, appetite and pleasure, just as our ancestors descended into Egypt, where they became slaves. And we too can become slaves to our physical desires. But when we enjoy our food with gratitude and holiness, rather than being controlled by the material world, we elevate it. That's our own little Exodus.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
348 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (During the meal we share words of Torah , bringing us to Mount Sinai. A thought on the parsha, a question, a story or a lively debate. The same Torah given thousands of years ago comes alive again at our table.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
349 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Finally, Birkat Hamazon , Grace After Meals, takes us into the future. We pray for Jerusalem to be rebuilt, for the return of the Temple and for the coming of Moshiach, when the world will finally fulfil its purpose.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (And so, in the space of one meal, we have travelled from Creation to Redemption. From Noach to Sodom, from Egypt to Sinai, from the beginning of history to its climax.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (But how do we get there? What moves the world from its messy beginnings to its ultimate glorious destination?)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (It's what happens around your table. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (The conversations with your kids. The guests you welcome. The Torah you share. The Jewish memories you create. The stories you tell. The values you pass on.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (We think of history as something that happens on a grand stage. But the real stage is our dining room. Even if the kids are fighting and the soup is spilling, you're still creating a Jewish home, passing on a tradition and building the future. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
